    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-002",
      "SourceSection": "10.2: How Many Books Can One Person Have?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The table shows the values of two functions, \\(P\\) and \\(B\\), where \\(P(t)\\) is the population of the US, in millions, \\(t\\) years after 2010, and \\(B(t)\\) is the number of books sold per year, in millions, \\(t\\) years after 2010.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-003",
      "SourceSection": "10.2: How Many Books Can One Person Have?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Plot the values of \\(B\\) as a function of \\(t\\). What does the plot tell you about book sales?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-004",
      "SourceSection": "10.2: How Many Books Can One Person Have?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How many books were sold per person in 2010 and 2016? What do these values tell you about book sales?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-005",
      "SourceSection": "10.2: How Many Books Can One Person Have?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Define a new function \\(R\\) by \\(R(t) = \\frac{B(t)}{P(t)}\\). Complete the table and then graph the values of \\(R(t)\\). What do the values of \\(R\\) tell you?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-006",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of two functions, \\(E\\) and \\(L\\). Define a new function \\(S\\) by adding \\(E\\) and \\(L\\), so \\(S(x) = E(x) + L(x)\\). On the same axes, sketch what you think the graph of \\(S\\) looks like.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sketch the graph of the sum of \\(E\\) and each of the following functions.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of two functions, \\(U\\) and \\(V\\). Define a new function \\(W\\) by multiplying \\(U\\) and \\(V\\), so \\(W(x) = U(x) V(x)\\). On the same axes, sketch what you think the graph of \\(W\\) looks like.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We can add, subtract, multiply, and divide functions to get new functions. For example, the cost in dollars of producing \\(n\\) cups of lemonade at a lemonade stand is \\(C(n) = 5 + 0.8n\\). The revenue (amount of money collected) from…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle P(n) = R(n) - C(n) = 2n - (5 + 0.8n) = 1.2n - 5\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of \\(C\\), \\(R\\), and \\(P\\). Can you see how each value on \\(P\\) is the result of the difference between the corresponding points on \\(R\\) and \\(C\\)?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The average profit per cup, \\(A(n)\\), from selling \\(n\\) cups, is the quotient of the profit and the number of cups, so",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle A(n) = \\frac{P(n)}{n} = \\frac{1.2n - 5}{n} = 1.2 - \\frac5n\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are the graphs of \\(P\\) and \\(A\\). Can you see how the value of \\(A(n)\\) is the result of the quotient of \\(P(n)\\) and \\(n\\)? Why does it make sense that both functions are negative when \\(n<4\\frac16\\) and positive when…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U05-L10-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Adding Functions",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Since \\(n\\) can only be positive, \\(P(n)\\) and \\(A(n)\\) always have the same sign for a given \\(n\\) value. Notice that for the average profit to be positive, the seller has to sell at least 5 cups (since \\(4\\frac16\\) is not in the domain,…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u05-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
